  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Arizona took control in the first inning and the Giants never mounted a recovery.",
    "what_this_means": "Robbie Ray lasted only 4.1 innings and gave up nine runs. The offense managed eight hits and converted one of six RISP situations into a run, but the damage came too early and ran too deep. By the fifth inning, Arizona had scored twelve of their final runs and the deficit was insurmountable. This is what a blowout looks like from the inside: early pressure, no moment to climb back, innings that pass without purpose. The Giants trailed from the start and never threatened to change that. One run on base runners is not an offensive failure by itself, but in a game decided by the third inning, missed chances in the fourth, seventh, and eighth felt like decisions already made.",
    "what_to_watch": "The trend has turned fragile. The team was stabilizing at 20-27 before this game; they are now 20-28 and 9.5 back in a division that does not wait for recovery windows. Ray's outing and the early deficit expose what happens when the starter cannot locate: the bullpen inherits a hole it cannot dig out of, and the offense never gets the breathing room to work. This is not a new problem. It is a familiar one getting louder.",
